# frozen_string_literal: true

# RegisteredTools — APP-OWNED, yours to edit.
#
# This is the ONE place that lists what the AI may call: the allow-list. McpController
# passes `RegisteredTools.all` to `MCP::Server` on every request, and the `mcp` gem
# lists and calls only the classes in that array — there is no generic executor and no
# console tool.
#
# To expose a tool, add its class to the array below. To stop exposing one, remove it.
# That is the whole registration model: an ordinary Ruby array of tool classes.
#
# `.all` is called per request so the classes are named fresh each time — safe across
# a Zeitwerk dev reload (no cached, stale class objects).
#
# WHAT CARRIES THE GUARANTEES: only a `RailsMcp::Tool` subclass that does NOT override
# `call` carries the authorize + audit + args allow-list guarantees — they all live in
# `RailsMcp::Tool.call`. Listing a bare `MCP::Tool`, or a subclass that overrides `call`,
# runs that tool UNAUDITED and UNGATED (no fail-closed authorize, no one-event audit, no
# undeclared-arg dropping). That is allowed — the gem neither warns nor blocks — but it is
# a deliberate, app-owned exception you take on knowingly. The stamped guard test
# (test/mcp/registered_tools_guard_test.rb) fails on such an entry until you list it in
# its ALLOWLISTED_RAW_TOOLS, so the exception shows up in a diff instead of silently.
module RegisteredTools
  def self.all
    [
      ExampleReadOnlyTool
    ]
  end
end
